From 7003f65f8617f99839e6318c217bba8e0a07cf42 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jackwang0009 <93422958+jackwang0009@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 12:34:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] [upstream-candidate] Write the graphics state parameter
 dictionaries back

The generator only wrote an ExtGState of its own making, with the fill
alpha, the stroke alpha and the blend mode of the object. Whatever else
the `gs` operators of the original content set was gone once the stream
was regenerated: soft masks, overprint (OP, op, OPM), text knockout,
stroke adjustment, transfer functions, black generation, halftones, and
any entry PDFium has no use for itself. The miter limit and the flatness
tolerance were lost as well, there was no code to write them.

Record the dictionaries with the general state, next to the resource
names that are recorded already, and write a `gs` operator for each of
them when the object is written. Nothing has to be known about an entry
to keep it this way.

- CPDF_GeneralState::AppendGraphicsResource() replaces
  AppendGraphicsResourceName(). It drops a recorded dictionary once the
  dictionaries applied after it set every one of its keys again, so the
  list stays as short as the number of different keys.
- The copy constructor of CPDF_GeneralState::StateData did not copy
  graphics_resource_names_. An object lost its names as soon as its
  general state was modified, and with them the claim on the resources.
- The content stream of a form starts with nothing recorded. The
  dictionaries the page applied are not resources of the form, and they
  apply by themselves whenever the page paints the form.
- A soft mask is laid out in the coordinate system that is current when
  its dictionary is applied. The object knows that matrix; when it is
  not the identity matrix, the dictionary is written as
  `<matrix> cm /Name gs <inverse> cm`. A text in a text clip group is
  written between BT and ET, where cm is not allowed, so such a text is
  not grouped.
- The line parameters and the flatness tolerance are written after the
  dictionaries, and always when one of the dictionaries sets them, so
  the operators keep the last word. `M` and `i` are new.
- Alpha and blend mode can be changed through the API. When they differ
  from what the recorded dictionaries leave them at, the default
  graphics dictionary and a dictionary for the new values follow, as
  before. Objects that were not parsed are written as before.
- A dictionary that is not in the resources of the holder is added to
  them under a new name (the object moved to another page), or, if it
  was removed as unused, gets its old name back.

Tests: CPDFPageContentGeneratorTest.ProcessExtGState,
ProcessExtGStateWithChangedAlpha, ProcessExtGStateOnAnotherPage,
ExtGStateOfInactiveObject, ProcessExtGStateInForm and
ProcessMiterLimitAndFlatness. pdfium_unittests and pdfium_embeddertests:
no new failures.

Verification (ops_diff.py, which now also compares TK, the soft mask
and its matrix, the remaining ExtGState entries, flatness, rendering
intent and the line parameters): differences in graphics state
parameters 9 -> 0 on the synthetic corpus and 178 -> 1 on the public
test files (SA 106, SM 33, TR 32, OPM 3, FL 2, AIS 1; the one left is
an `ri` operator, which the parser ignores). No-diff pages 61/67 ->
63/67 synthetic (gs-line-params, gs-smask-luminosity), 384/388 ->
384/388 public. Two new samples, gs-smask-ctm and
gs-stacked-other-keys, differed in pixels and in 35 parameters before
and are pixel-identical now.

